## Why 1.25ct Marquise Beats 1.5ct Round at Half the Lab Price

A 1.5ct round diamond measures 7.4 mm across — face-up area of 43 mm². A 1.25ct marquise measures 11.0 × 5.5 mm — face-up area of 47.5 mm². The marquise wins on area by 10%. The marquise wins on length by 49%. The round costs 3–5x more at this spec on the lab market.

I show this comparison to clients who come in asking for "the biggest ring my budget can buy." Half of them walk out with 1.25ct marquise lab. The face-up size reads bigger than the carat weight suggests. The money saved goes into a halo or three-stone setting that pushes perceived ring value another $2,000–$3,000 up.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>1.25ct Marquise (Lab)</th><th>1.5ct Round (Lab)</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Face-up (mm)</strong></td><td>11.0 × 5.5</td><td>7.4 Ø</td><td>Marquise is 49% longer than the round's diameter.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Face-up area (mm²)</strong></td><td>47.5</td><td>43.0</td><td>Marquise wins area by 10%.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Starting price (lab)</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29754536?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$792</a></td><td>$2,500+</td><td>1.25ct marquise lab costs 32% of 1.5ct round lab.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Reads as</strong></td><td>"Dramatic engagement ring"</td><td>"Classic 1.5ct ring"</td><td>Both read as premium rings. Marquise reads as more distinctive.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** For "biggest-looking ring per dollar" at this budget, 1.25ct marquise lab beats 1.5ct round lab. Pay less, get more face-up area, get a more distinctive silhouette. Full shape comparison in our [round diamond vs marquise](/round-diamond-vs-marquise) guide.

The one trade-off is finger coverage side-to-side. A 1.5ct round covers 45% of a size-6 finger across. A 1.25ct marquise covers 33% side-to-side but 67% lengthwise. If side-to-side width matters more to you (common on size 7+ fingers), round wins. If length matters more (common on size 5–6), marquise wins. Both are honest buys. The choice comes down to how your partner wears rings on the hand.

## How to Verify a 1.25 Carat Marquise Before You Buy

Three checks every stone must pass before you add to cart.

**Check 1 — The certificate measurements.** For a 1.25ct marquise at 2.00 L:W, measurements should read close to 11.0 × 5.5 × 3.4 mm. If length is below 10.6 mm, the stone is cut deep and wasting carat weight below the girdle. More in our [marquise ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

**Check 2 — The HD video for bow-tie severity.** Open the 360° HD video on the retailer listing. Look at the center of the stone face-up. A faint shadow is normal. A hard dark band across the middle is a bow-tie — walk away. Full grading in our [marquise bow-tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).

**Check 3 — Polish and symmetry both at "Very Good" or higher.** The lab does not give an overall cut grade for marquise. These two sub-grades are what you have. If either reads "Good" or lower, the long parallel facets will show visible asymmetry at 11 mm face-up. Reject.

I run these three checks on every 1.25ct marquise before I quote a client. The Lab-Only Window at this size means budget-first buyers already win on price — but the stone still has to pass these three checks or the ring falls flat regardless of grade.

